


The Data Stack

            An interface provides access to several stacks,
            one for each core.

            The initial argument of each access functions is
            an int that specifies which stack is being
            referenced.

    Functions

            The stack_reset function removes all data from
            the stack and initializes an internal stack
            pointer.

            The stack_length function returns the number of
            elements on the stack.

            The stack_room function returns the number of
            elements which could be added to the stack.

            The stack_peek function returns the value of the
            element on the top of the stack if there are any
            stored elements. Otherwise, the stack_peek
            function returns a negative value.

            The stack_pull function behaves much the same as
            the stack_peek function, except that if there
            are any elements on the stack, the top element
            is removed.

            The stack_push function pushes the value of #1
            onto the stack if there is room. The stack_push
            function returns the value pushed, or, if the
            stack is full, a negative value.

            The stack_dump function writes to the standard
            error stream a representation of the stack's
            data. Elements closer to the top of the stack
            are written before elements closer to the bottom
            of the stack.
